  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nWe introduce a novel method based on alternating optimization between the primal variable (coefficients) and the dual variable (reset values) under a sequence of linear constraints, inspired by the [dual-feasible-set] but robustly applied. This approach ensures that the search always achieves arithmetic progression reset simultaneously, minimizing the developmental surface of unconstrained violation while leveraging the strong results of the previous LP-based method. Specifically, we optimize the objective $J = 2n \\max(convolve(a,a)) / \\left(\\sum a\\right)^2$ by partitioning the problem into two sets: (1) direct LP updates and (2) constrained-projection via dual variables. The kernel innovation bounds are enforced using momentum and normalized positions, ensuring only sequences meeting the criteria $0 \\leq a_i \\leq 1000$ are accepted, while internal updates escape enforcement. This dovetails with the previous program but introduces a streamlined convexification step using finite-differences.\n\nMathematical Specification:\nApproximate the gradient of $J$ using finite-differences, but project into the dual feasible set $[d_{min}, d_{max}]$. Maintain the current sequence and its objective. For each position $i$, with coefficient $a_i$, compute the finite-difference gradient $\\nabla_i J$ by perturbing $a_i$ (capped at 1000) in the direction that minimizes $J$. The boundary conditions (0 and 1000) are treated as concentration points. Formulate the dual as a reset vector for arithmetic progression, solving the LP update with adaptive scaling based on the gradient magnitude. \n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Initial Setup:\n   - Set $n=800$, initialize $a$ using the parent (height_sequence_1) or uniform initialization.\n   - Compute initial clipped sequence and base objective.\n\n2. Iterative Loop:\n   - Precompute the linear convolution (convolve mode full) for the current sequence once.\n   - For each of 1000 iterations: mutate a portion (variable) of the sequence based on 1.5*n steps using a momentum gradient update, but only 8 positions per iteration try a new dual variable update.\n   - Update steps: first perturb unclipped to get gradient, then update via projected dual variable to escape enforcement boundaries.\n\n3. Core Update:\n   - A partitioned convex update: for each element in $\\mathit{core} = \\{0 \\leq i \\leq 500 \\mid 2i + J[i] > \\sum a \\}$, compute gradient $\\nabla_i J$ using finite-differences respecting elemental caps.\n   - Create dual vector $g$ with step size $t$ (best in 0.1 to 0.9 decreases) to reset arithmetic constraints.\n\n4. Candidate Acceptance:\n   - Run evaluate_sequence once after each significant update.\n   - Store best candidate (lowest $J$) found during the entire search.\n\n5. Final Reset:\n   - In the final proposal, rescan all elements with a thrice-fine step (1/20) of the original to fetch final polished candidates.\n\nComputation Budget:\n- Assume one convolution computation is O(2*n^2) operations (n=800), 2*n^2 = 1.28e4 steps -> 1.5e6 operations.\n- Each iteration does 1/7 of all elements. \n  Each convolution involves n^2 operations (\u22480.5 seconds), but we do one convolution initial, and then for new candidates: 8 evaluations (each of which may involve indirected convolutions but we rely on the evaluator for safety).\n- Time limit: 1500 seconds; focus optimization on core elements while others use random moves.\n\nSearch Choices and Useful Variations:\n- Use gamma momentum for elements not in core.\n- Core elements run dual-vector projection steps for better convex relaxation.\n- Alternate between active set and global positions randomly.\n\nValidation and Final Return:\n- After the loop, evaluate the best candidate once more with evaluate_sequence and return.\n- Ensure that invalid sequences (sum<0.01, negative, infinite, NaN, etc.) are rejected violently.\n- If no candidate found, use fallback sequence (all ones) to avoid np.inf.\n\nFallback: Use all those records try each. If nothing found, use uniform initialization.",
